1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,144
Presenter:

2
00:00:01,144 --> 00:00:06,021
My guest on the show today is Alice Riches who started the Street Play

3
00:00:06,021 --> 00:00:08,510
Scheme where she lives in Beechwood Road.

4
00:00:08,510 --> 00:00:13,055
For those of you that don't already know – Street Play involves local residents

5
00:00:13,055 --> 00:00:15,810
closing off their street for a few hours

6
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,143
so that children have a chance to play in the street safely.

7
00:00:19,143 --> 00:00:23,320
She started it in her own street, Beechwood Road,

8
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:24,795
and the idea caught on,

9
00:00:24,795 --> 00:00:28,040
and there are now Street Play Schemes all over the city.

10
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:31,710
So when did you actually start the scheme, Alice?

11
00:00:31,710 --> 00:00:36,604
Alice:Well, I first had the idea when my oldest child was still a

12
00:00:36,604 --> 00:00:36,953
toddler,

13
00:00:36,953 --> 00:00:39,400
so that's about six years ago now –

14
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:43,359
but it took at least two years of campaigning before we were actually able

15
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:44,490
to make it happen.

16
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:47,890
So the scheme's been up and running for three years now.

17
00:00:47,890 --> 00:00:52,050
We'd love to be able to close our road for longer – for the

18
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:55,250
whole weekend, from Saturday morning until Sunday evening,for example.

19
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:57,626
At the moment it's just once a week.

20
00:00:57,626 --> 00:01:00,595
But when we started it was only once a month.

21
00:01:00,595 --> 00:01:02,080
But we're working on it.

22
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:02,415
Presenter:

23
00:01:02,415 --> 00:01:05,430
So what actually happens when Beechwood Road is closed?

24
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:10,266
Alice:We have volunteer wardens, mostly parents but some elderly residents too,

25
00:01:10,266 --> 00:01:13,490
who block off our road at either end.

26
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:19,790
The council have provided special signs but there's always a volunteer there to explain

27
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:22,040
what's happening to any motorists.

28
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:27,841
Generally, they're fine about it – we've only had to get the police involved once

29
00:01:27,841 --> 00:01:28,670
or twice.

30
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:33,890
Now I should explain that the road isn't completely closed to cars.

31
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,600
But only residents' cars are allowed.

32
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:41,593
If people really need to get in or out of Beechwood Road, it's not

33
00:01:41,593 --> 00:01:46,230
a problem – as long as they drive at under 20 kilometres per hour.

34
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:51,088
But most people just decide not to use their cars during this time,

35
00:01:51,088 --> 00:01:53,330
or they park in another street.

36
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:57,000
The wardens are only there to stop through traffic.

37
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:57,334
Presenter:

38
00:01:57,334 --> 00:02:00,670
So can anyone apply to get involved in Street Play?

39
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:05,971
Alice:Absolutely – we want to include all kids in the city – especially those

40
00:02:05,971 --> 00:02:08,010
who live on busy roads.

41
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:10,710
It's here that demand is greatest.

42
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:17,330
Obviously, there isn't such demand in wealthier areas where the children have access to parks

43
00:02:17,330 --> 00:02:20,420
or large gardens – or in the suburbs

44
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:24,270
where there are usually more places for children to play outside.

45
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:29,347
I'd recommend that anyone listening who likes the idea should just give it a

46
00:02:29,347 --> 00:02:29,710
go.

47
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:34,924
We've been surprised by the positive reaction of residents all over the city.

48
00:02:34,924 --> 00:02:36,930
And that's not just parents.

49
00:02:36,930 --> 00:02:41,740
There are always a few who complain but they're a tiny minority.

50
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:46,678
On the whole everyone is very supportive and say they're very happy to see

51
00:02:46,678 --> 00:02:50,910
children out on the street – even if it does get quite noisy.

52
00:02:50,910 --> 00:02:57,750
Alice:There have been so many benefits of Street Play for the kids.

53
00:02:57,750 --> 00:03:02,622
Parents really like the fact that the kids are getting fresh air instead of

54
00:03:02,622 --> 00:03:04,710
sitting staring at a computer screen,

55
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:07,970
even if they're not doing anything particularly energetic.

56
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:12,988
And of course it's great that kids can play with their friends outside without

57
00:03:12,988 --> 00:03:14,780
being supervised by their parents –

58
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:20,844
but for me the biggest advantage is that kids develop confidence in themselves to

59
00:03:20,844 --> 00:03:23,010
be outside without their parents.

60
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:28,695
The other really fantastic thing is that children get to know the adults in

61
00:03:28,695 --> 00:03:32,350
the street – it's like having a big extended family.

62
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:32,644
Presenter:

63
00:03:32,644 --> 00:03:34,996
It certainly does have a lot of benefits.

64
00:03:34,996 --> 00:03:39,112
I want to move on now and ask you about a related project in

65
00:03:39,112 --> 00:03:39,700
King Street.

66
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:40,433
Alice:Right.

67
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:45,933
Well this was an experiment I was involved in where local residents decided to try

68
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:48,500
and reduce the traffic along King Street,

69
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:53,267
which is the busiest main road in our area, by persuading people not to

70
00:03:53,267 --> 00:03:55,310
use their cars for one day.

71
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:59,191
We thought about making people pay more for parking –

72
00:03:59,191 --> 00:04:02,640
but we decided that would be really unpopular –

73
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:06,270
so instead we just stopped people from parking on King Street

74
00:04:06,270 --> 00:04:08,580
but left the other car parks open.

75
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:12,460
It was surprising how much of a difference all this made.

76
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:18,795
As we'd predicted, air quality was significantly better but what I hadn't expected was

77
00:04:18,795 --> 00:04:21,510
how much quieter it would be –

78
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:23,900
even with the buses still running.

79
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:28,705
Of course everyone said they felt safer but we were actually amazed that sales

80
00:04:28,705 --> 00:04:31,450
in the shops went up considerably that day –

81
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:35,140
we thought there'd be fewer people out shopping – not more.

82
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:35,524
Presenter:

83
00:04:35,524 --> 00:04:38,210
That's really interesting so the fact that …
